<h2>The petition</h2>
<p>The petitioners, both adults, married without the consent of the first petitioner&rsquo;s parents and registered the marriage. The immediate source of apprehension was concrete: her father had arranged her marriage to another man, fixed for January 2027, and the couple feared the consequences of having pre-empted it. They approached the High Court under its writ jurisdiction seeking protection of their life and liberty.</p>

<h2>How the hearing resolved it</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The father, heard.</strong> He appeared before the Court and stated that he did not intend to harm either petitioner — an assurance now on the judicial record, with all that implies for any future breach.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The police, bound.</strong> Police Station Prem Nagar accepted notice, and the Court directed the police to ensure that no harm comes to the petitioners.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The couple, structured.</strong> Before visiting the parental home, the petitioners must give advance intimation to the local police station. They stated their intention to live in Mathura.</div>
</div>
<p>With these three pieces in place, the petition was disposed of without a formal protection order — the protective architecture residing instead in the recorded assurance and the standing direction to the police.</p>

<h2>The legal foundation</h2>
<p>The right of an adult to marry a person of their own choice is an incident of personal liberty under Article 21, and constitutional courts have consistently enforced it against family and community opposition. The writ court&rsquo;s toolkit in these cases is deliberately graduated. At one end lies the formal protection order with police security; at the other, orders like the present one, where an assurance in court, a direction to the SHO, and sensible ground-rules achieve safety without escalation. The choice between them is driven by the threat picture: specific, demonstrated threats attract firmer orders, while generalised apprehension in the aftermath of a family conflict is often best met with recorded undertakings and police accountability.</p>

<h2>What couples in this position should know</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Step</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Register the marriage</td><td>A registered marriage certificate forecloses disputes about the fact of marriage and the parties&rsquo; ages</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Preserve proof of age and consent</td><td>Birth records and identity documents answer the commonest counter-allegations at the threshold</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Document threats</td><td>Dates, messages, witnesses — specificity determines the strength of the protection ordered</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Approach the court promptly</td><td>Protection petitions work best before confrontation, not after; the court can bind the police in advance</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Keep contact structured</td><td>If visiting the family, follow any intimation conditions scrupulously; they exist for the couple&rsquo;s safety and keep the record clean</td></tr>
</table>
<p>For families, the message of orders like this one is equally plain: assurances given in court are not courtesies. A breach converts a quietly disposed-of writ petition into contempt proceedings and criminal liability, with the earlier record already made.</p>
<div class="note">This note summarises an order of the Delhi High Court for general information and is not advice on any particular matter.</div>
